many people ask me questions about stoic  philosophy and practice I've created  this series to provide short answers to  the most common recurring or confusing  questions about stoicism does being a  stoic mean that I can't have empathy  this is a really great question and I  think it's one that we need to be  careful in answering to make a number of  needed distinctions for those who don't  want to wait around for that discussion  there is a very simple answer no being a  stoic does not mean that you cannot have  empathy as a matter of fact there are  plenty of people who either  self-identify as Stoics or we can call  Stoics because they are classical  representatives who did display what we  nowadays call empathy before we  discussed why it is that yes a stoic can  in fact feel and exercise empathy  towards other people we should think a  little bit about some of the assumptions  that are going into this and and one  thing to clarify from the beginning not  everybody means the same thing by  empathy and we'll talk about that in  just a moment  but why are people so concerned about  this why would they think that being a  stoic would automatically preclude that  I think that one of the reasons is a  misunderstanding of what it means to be  a stoic there's often a distinction made  between lowercase s stoicism which is a  character trait or way of being it's  sort of a popular conception and then  there's actual uppercase stoicism what  it is that stoic philosophers actually  taught and what it is that Stoics in the  present actually teach and practice  themselves today and I think the the  more generic popular misconception of  lowercase s stoicism does in fact rule  out empathy you want to detach yourself  as much as possible from other people  and their feelings or their suffering  but that's not  actually real stoicism genuine stoicism  says that we ought to be concerned about  others but we ought not to be concerned  in a sort of willy-nilly arbitrary way  we should do so in a rational way now  speaking about empathizing in a rational  way I think is going to throw a lot of  people because they have the mistaken  assumption that somehow rationality and  feeling affect emotion are two totally  separate things and they have nothing to  do with each other there are some  viewpoints that hold that stoicism is  not one of them as a matter of fact the  ideal stoic sage the person that we  rarely see but also you know what it is  that a person in development or working  on themselves ought to tend towards is  going to feel some things towards other  people these are called the you path I  the good emotions if you like or good  effects and empathy in the way that many  people understand it today could in fact  be among those effects I'd like to  mention just a few examples from classic  stoic texts and thinkers so Epictetus in  talking about why people do things wrong  and whether we should you know get upset  with them he says that perhaps we would  be better off and it's translated  feeling pity and that term there allows  can also be translated as compassion and  I think that it has some of the same  semantic range as empathy particularly  sense  Epictetus suggests that we you know  might want to think about why the person  is is doing the wrongheaded things that  they're doing that may make us angry or  may make us feel sad or produce other  emotions and that is in a certain sense  to empathize with them he also mentions  at another point when it comes to  grieving it's perfectly fine to sit  there and put your arm around somebody  and say oh this is really bad he says  just don't groan inwardly so that's sort  of a rational approach to it Marcus  Aurelius throughout the  patience as many similar things I think  you can just dig around and there you're  gonna find him saying you know think  about what you would feel like if you  were in that situation and then you know  maybe don't be so judgy right and so  when we get down to it we see these are  just a few examples there are many  examples of Stoics actually counselling  or displaying well we would nowadays  call by this slippery term empathy bring  this to a close there is no  incompatibility between being a stoic  whether in the sense of being the ideal  develops age or being somebody who's  committed to stoicism and practicing it  and trying to move towards that there's  no contradiction or incompatibility  between that and empathy understood in  the proper sense as a matter of fact you  can actually make a case that some  version of empathy is probably going to  be integral to the stoic life if you  found this answer helpful for you then  please share it with others who might  benefit from it this work is entirely  supported by crowdfunding on my patreon  site so if you find it valuable consider  becoming a supporter at patreon.com  slash Sadler
 

